<div class="section" id="g_t_041c_0430_0440_0448_0440_0443_0442_0438_0437_0438_0440_0443_0435_043c_0430_044f-_0441_0435_0442_044c-_0434_043b_044f-libvirt">
<div class="header">
<p>
Next: <a href="Polxzovatelxskaya-setx-NAT-dlya-libvirt.html" accesskey="n" rel="next">Пользовательская сеть NAT для libvirt</a>, Previous: <a href="Setevyi-most-dlya-QEMU.html" accesskey="p" rel="prev">Сетевый мост для QEMU</a>, Up: <a href="Virtualxnye-mashiny.html" accesskey="u" rel="up">Виртуальные машины</a> &nbsp; [<a href="index.html#SEC_Contents" title="Table of contents" rel="contents">Contents</a>][<a href="Terminy-i-ukazateli.html" title="Index" rel="index">Index</a>]</p>
</div>
<hr />
<span id="Marshrutiziruemaya-setx-dlya-libvirt"></span><h3 class="section">5.2 Маршрутизируемая сеть для libvirt</h3>
<span id="index-Virtual-network-bridge-interface"></span>
<span id="index-networking_002c-virtual-bridge"></span>
<span id="index-libvirt_002c-virtual-network-switch"></span>

<p>If the machine hosting your virtual machines is connected wirelessly to the
network, you won&rsquo;t be able to use a true network bridge as explained in the
preceding section (see <a href="Setevyi-most-dlya-QEMU.html">Сетевый мост для QEMU</a>).  In this case, the next
best option is to use a <em>virtual network switch</em> with static routing
and to configure a libvirt-powered virtual machine to use it (via the
<code>virt-manager</code> GUI for example).  This is similar to the default
mode of operation of QEMU/libvirt, except that instead of using <abbr title="Network Address Translation">NAT</abbr> (Network Address Translation), it relies on static routes to join the
<abbr title="virtual machine">VM</abbr> (virtual machine) IP address to the <abbr title="local area
network">LAN</abbr> (local area
network).  This provides two-way connectivity to and from the virtual
machine, which is needed for exposing services hosted on the virtual
machine.
</p>
<ul class="section-toc">
<li><a href="#Creating-a-virtual-network-switch" accesskey="1">Creating a virtual network switch</a></li>
<li><a href="#Configuring-the-static-routes-for-your-virtual-bridge" accesskey="2">Configuring the static routes for your virtual bridge</a></li>
</ul>
<div class="subsection" id="Creating-a-virtual-network-switch">
<h4 class="subsection">5.2.1 Creating a virtual network switch</h4>

<p>A virtual network switch consists of a virtual network device called a
&lsquo;virtual bridge&rsquo;, DHCP server (dnsmasq) and firewall rules (iptables). See
the <a href="https://wiki.libvirt.org/VirtualNetworking.html">libvirt Wiki
article on Virtual Networking</a> for more details on the modes of operation,
management and implementation of virtual network switches.
</p>
<p>The <code>virsh</code> command, provided by the <code>libvirt</code> package, makes
it very easy to create a virtual switch.  You first need to choose a network
subnet for your virtual switch; if your home LAN is in the
&lsquo;<samp>192.168.1.0/24</samp>&rsquo; network, you could opt to use e.g.
&lsquo;<samp>192.168.2.0/24</samp>&rsquo;.  Define an XML file, e.g. <samp>/tmp/virbr0.xml</samp>,
containing the following:
</p>
<div class="example">
<pre class="example">&lt;network&gt;
  &lt;name&gt;virbr0&lt;/name&gt;
  &lt;bridge name="virbr0" /&gt;
  &lt;forward mode="route"/&gt;
  &lt;ip address="192.168.2.0" netmask="255.255.255.0"&gt;
    &lt;dhcp&gt;
      &lt;range start="192.168.2.1" end="192.168.2.254"/&gt;
    &lt;/dhcp&gt;
  &lt;/ip&gt;
&lt;/network&gt;
</pre></div>

<p>Then create and configure the interface using the <code>virsh</code> command,
as root:
</p>
<div class="example">
<pre class="example">virsh net-define /tmp/virbr0.xml
virsh net-autostart virbr0
virsh net-start virbr0
</pre></div>

<p>The &lsquo;<samp>virbr0</samp>&rsquo; interface should now be visible e.g. via the &lsquo;<samp>ip
address</samp>&rsquo; command.  It will be automatically started every time your libvirt
virtual machine is started.
</p>
</div>
<div class="subsection" id="Configuring-the-static-routes-for-your-virtual-bridge">
<h4 class="subsection">5.2.2 Configuring the static routes for your virtual bridge</h4>

<p>If you configured your virtual machine to use your newly created
&lsquo;<samp>virbr0</samp>&rsquo; virtual bridge interface, it should already receive an IP via
DHCP such as &lsquo;<samp>192.168.2.15</samp>&rsquo; and be reachable from the server hosting
it, e.g. via &lsquo;<samp>ping 192.168.2.15</samp>&rsquo;.  There&rsquo;s one last configuration
needed so that the VM can reach the external network: adding static routes
to the network&rsquo;s router.
</p>
<p>In this example, the LAN network is &lsquo;<samp>192.168.1.0/24</samp>&rsquo; and the router
configuration web page may be accessible via e.g. the
<a href="http://192.168.1.1">http://192.168.1.1</a> page.  On a router running the
<a href="https://librecmc.org/">libreCMC</a> firmware, you would navigate to the
Network &rarr; Static Routes page
(<a href="https://192.168.1.1/cgi-bin/luci/admin/network/routes">https://192.168.1.1/cgi-bin/luci/admin/network/routes</a>), and you would
add a new entry to the &lsquo;<samp>Static IPv4 Routes</samp>&rsquo; with the following
information:
</p>
<dl compact="compact">
<dt><span>&lsquo;<samp>Интерфейс</samp>&rsquo;</span></dt>
<dd><p>lan
</p></dd>
<dt><span>&lsquo;<samp>Цель</samp>&rsquo;</span></dt>
<dd><p>192.168.2.0
</p></dd>
<dt><span>&lsquo;<samp>Маска IPv4</samp>&rsquo;</span></dt>
<dd><p>255.255.255.0
</p></dd>
<dt><span>&lsquo;<samp>Шлюз IPv4</samp>&rsquo;</span></dt>
<dd><p><var>server-ip</var>
</p></dd>
<dt><span>&lsquo;<samp>Route type</samp>&rsquo;</span></dt>
<dd><p>unicast
</p></dd>
</dl>

<p>where <var>server-ip</var> is the IP address of the machine hosting the VMs,
which should be static.
</p>
<p>After saving/applying this new static route, external connectivity should
work from within your VM; you can e.g. run &lsquo;<samp>ping gnu.org</samp>&rsquo; to verify
that it functions correctly.
</p>
</div>
</div>
